{"kind":"request","text":"Hugh: the gs-usability design decision, now revised to include the short event-hash input you asked about, and this time addressed to you so it is actually in your queue. Proposal d54de200 (replaces 7a2d2ea4) has two parts. (A) ID resolution: gs and the MCP accept a #N sequence or an unambiguous event-hash prefix/suffix and resolve it to the full canonical id at the tool boundary before signing — so you can paste back the short id the display shows. The invariants keep it safe: resolve only at the boundary and always sign the full 40-char id (a prefix unique today can collide as the log grows); scope the uniqueness scan to durable events, not git's whole object store; refuse loudly with candidates on ambiguity, never guessing, and never conflating that with the fold's silence-admits contract; echo the resolved id on writes. The fold is unchanged. (B) unchanged from before: warn at the CLI (not refuse in the fold) when a --rests-on resolves to nothing, coordinating with the in-flight admission work. Ratify to adopt, or dissent with what to change; on adoption I file the implementation request and the codex cheap-wins lane 1e9d35ab still proceeds first.","body":{"conditions":"Ratify proposal d54de200 (gs-usability id-resolution + submit-time basis validation, revised to make the short event-hash input form first-class) to adopt it, or record a dissent naming what to change. On adoption, planner files the implementation request; resolution stays at the CLI/MCP boundary with the full canonical id always signed, and the fold is unchanged.","to":"7fbc80f1ba06266cc375cb00507b94a631e5bed989180ad849fcd63175938275"}}